foreign  this is your Tech news briefing for  Friday October 28th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  all this week we've been bringing you  some of the most interesting and  thought-provoking discussions from the  Wall Street journal's Tech live  conference  to wrap up the week we recorded a  special episode in front of a live  audience outside in Laguna Beach  California  I sat down with wsj tech reporter Tim  Higgins to discuss a key theme from the  conference why self-driving technology  hasn't lived up to the hype at least not  as quickly as promised case in point  Intel spun out its self-driving car unit  mobilei this week valuing it at 17  billion dollars that is more than it  paid for the company but far less than  the 50 billion Intel had hoped for  so why have mobileye and others in the  autonomous driving space struggled here  are highlights from that conversation  with Tim Tim I want to start with Intel  Pat gelsinger was here at Tech live on  Monday and he was asked about the  company's plans to spin out mobileye  Intel brought mobilei to an IPO at a  significantly lower valuation than was  expected what went wrong  yeah I mean this is kind of maybe a  canary in the coal mine if you will  about the state of autonomous vehicle  technology out there mobile I uh it  produces the kind of the brains for  driver assist programs out there for  vehicles but also is working on fully  self-driving technology and they were  hoping to IPO at a valuation of 50  billion and now it's dramatically less  hey you've got an issue that the market  for IPOs is just it's a horrible time to  be out there but there's also concern  among investors that I have talked to  just about kind of the state of  autonomous vehicle technology is this  actually going to happen in the near  term and where does mobileye play in  that you know this is something this  kind of Technology not just mobileye but  this the state of driverless cars we  have been talking about it for more than  a decade and it's always kind of been  something that was near term it was just  on the horizon but it's proven time and  time again to be harder than everybody  initially anticipated and I think some  people's patience is waning for it okay  then where does the state of driverless  technology stand at the moment well it's  not Knight Rider you know the the the  famous uh self-driving car crime fighter  sure it's more nuanced and more limited  you can take a fully self-driving car in  Phoenix right now using waymo which is  uh Google's self-driving uh subsidiary  but you know it's this is limited uh you  can't go everywhere out there and  they're slowly rolling out this service  they're going to San Francisco and they  just announced Los Angeles and what  we've found and what they have found is  that there's a huge back-end component  to this technology you gotta you gotta  charge the vehicle you got to put  windshield wiper fluid you got to clean  it out if somebody leaves their purse or  you know they vomit in it or whatever  you got this huge infrastructure uh back  there which  I tend to call that vomit problem  because you think that the idea of when  this technology was first talked about  years ago was these role this is all  going to be automated and robots are  going to be taking over and it was just  going to be so easy you could just  deploy these things well it actually  requires an army of people behind the  scenes to keep the logistics going and  that's one of the big surprises I think  that the industry has dealt with in  recent years is trying to figure out  that complexity you spoke with the waymo  co-ceo uh here at Tech live on Monday we  actually have a clip of what she said in  reaction to what it is to get these cars  on the road this is really about being  patient in the learning but being very  precise in our execution of what makes  this a delightful service  so a delightful service do we have an  idea of when they're going to be able to  turn this into a more accessible  delightful service it's a city by City  kind of problem for them I mean think  about me as a driver you can plot me  down in Phoenix or I can go to San  Francisco or Los Angeles and I can get  off the plane and just start driving but  for them their driver which is how they  call their their service has to be tuned  for all those markets it has to be  trained the infrastructure has to be  built around it and it's taking time to  do that so it's clear that getting  faster at it but it's also it's more  than an engineering problem we talked  about logistics but there's a political  issue in Phoenix they were able to  easily roll out or they they rolled it  out but in California there's a level of  bureaucracies involved that they have to  have permissions from various places and  that takes time you know that's that's  such a good point and Pat gelsinger who  was also here at Tech live he spoke  about that a little bit uh when he  talked about ipoing mobile eye I think  we have a clip from him on regulation as  well well many other cities and  countries around the world are much more  advanced than the regulatory policies  enabling autonomous vehicles you know to  emerge more rapidly and clearly you know  to me you know not only do we need  things like chips and science act to  move things forward but we also need  regulatory policies to enable  Technologies to come forward so Tim does  that gel with what you've been hearing  from other autonomous driving companies  you know it's interesting for years  writing about this there's always been  this belief that self-driving technology  will have an easier time deploying in  China just because the government could  turn it on if they wanted to it's not  necessarily the case for U.S companies  given that these are essentially mobile  map making machines and there's concerns  about National Security there so there's  ego Market to Market there's different  complexities and there's different  logistical hurdles if you will here in  the states  the challenge has been traditionally the  federal government oversees the car and  the states oversee the driver and so the  question becomes when the car is the  driver who's who's in charge there a  company like waymo would like it to be  at a federal level so you could have one  rule to rule at all here in the states  uh where you know we're still kind of in  early days of local governments uh  deciding how they want to weigh in  environment and  then expected mean for investors in this  space it's all kind of that level of  complexity and risk that just increases  we saw in 2018 the first death of a  person involved was hit by a test  driving self-driving car uh in Phoenix  area by Uber at the time and that really  put the industry back it all of a sudden  shook a lot of people is that you know  this if it wasn't already clear we are  dealing with uh life and death  technology and that gives people some  pause you know how do you know if the  system works how do you know if the car  that's being put on the road is safe Tim  Higgins wsj reporter thank you so much  for joining us thank you  [Applause]  and that's it for Tech news briefing  this week our producer is Julie Chang  our supervising producer is Chris  sinsley and I'm your host Zoe Thomas  thanks for listening and have a great  weekend  [Music]  [Music]

